---
title: "JSON output and exit codes"
description: "Machine-readable results, the stable error envelope, debug traces, and exit codes."
---

Every command accepts the global `--json` flag. With it, stdout holds exactly
**one** JSON object: the result, or the error when the command fails. Progress,
browser instructions, warnings, and suggested next commands go to stderr as
text, so a result never has a `next` field.

```sh
photon --json whoami
photon auth status --json
photon project list --json
```

<Tabs>
  <Tab title="Terminal">
    ```console
    $ photon project list
    NAME             ID                                  SLUG            ACTIVE  UPDATED
    Support line     pho_prj_01k9yq3m7d2c8x4v6b5n0p1r2s  support-line    *       2026-08-01T18:00:00.000Z
    Billing alerts   pho_prj_01k9yq3m7d2c8x4v6b5n0p1r2t  billing-alerts          2026-08-03T18:00:00.000Z
    ```
  </Tab>
  <Tab title="JSON">
    ```console
    $ photon project list --json
    {"projects":[{"agentProfile":{"avatarUrl":"https://images.example.com/agent.png","firstName":"Photon","lastName":null},"billingInitialization":"completed","createdAt":"2026-08-01T18:00:00.000Z","createdByAccountId":"pho_acc_01k9yq3m7d2c8x4v6b5n0p1r2s","createdByActorId":"pho_acc_01k9yq3m7d2c8x4v6b5n0p1r2s","createdByActorKind":"account","deletedAt":null,"name":"Support line","organizationId":"pho_org_01k9yq3m7d2c8x4v6b5n0p1r2s","projectId":"pho_prj_01k9yq3m7d2c8x4v6b5n0p1r2s","slug":"support-line","updatedAt":"2026-08-01T18:00:00.000Z"},{"agentProfile":{"avatarUrl":"https://images.example.com/agent.png","firstName":"Photon","lastName":null},"billingInitialization":"completed","createdAt":"2026-08-03T18:00:00.000Z","createdByAccountId":"pho_acc_01k9yq3m7d2c8x4v6b5n0p1r2s","createdByActorId":"pho_acc_01k9yq3m7d2c8x4v6b5n0p1r2s","createdByActorKind":"account","deletedAt":null,"name":"Billing alerts","organizationId":"pho_org_01k9yq3m7d2c8x4v6b5n0p1r2s","projectId":"pho_prj_01k9yq3m7d2c8x4v6b5n0p1r2t","slug":"billing-alerts","updatedAt":"2026-08-03T18:00:00.000Z"}]}
    ```
  </Tab>
</Tabs>

Without `--json`, stdout also holds only the answer. A list with nothing in it
prints its message (for example `No projects found.`) on stderr, a list with
more pages ends with a `More results:` line on stderr that names the command for
the next page, and a declined confirmation prints `Cancelled.` on stderr and
exits `1`.

Only `--json` prints JSON. Without it the output is text, at a terminal or not:
off a terminal the text drops color and spinners but says the same thing.
Piping the output doesn't switch to JSON, and no environment variable or
configuration setting chooses the format. Every `--help` ends with a line that
tells agents and scripts to add `--json`.

The format never changes what a command does. A command that waits, such as
`project platform email connect` or `project platform telegram connect`, waits
with or without `--json`; only `--wait` and `--no-wait` change that. With
`--json`, what it prints before it waits goes to stderr, so stdout holds only
the final JSON object.

A spinner shows on stderr only when stdout and stderr are both terminals, `CI`
isn't on, `TERM` isn't `dumb`, and there's no `--json`.

## Envelopes

Most commands print the API's response as Photon sends it, as described below.
The documents the CLI defines are in the table further down. Some put the
API's object under a name of their own, such as `project`, `organization` or
`operation`, and others add keys beside the API's response, such as
`provisioning`.

Paginated collections carry the API's token for the next page under the API's
name for it: `nextPageToken` when more records remain, `nextCursor` for
invoices and `listMetadata.after` for OAuth connections, both `null` on the
last page. A list that keeps the API's envelope, as described below, and whose
`--limit` takes more than one page holds every page's items, the last page's
`nextPageToken` when more records remain, and the last page's other top-level
fields. Of the documents the CLI defines, the ones that say where the CLI found
or saved a selection, or where the sign-in comes from, add `source`. When
there's no API body to print, because Photon sends none or the command makes
no API call, the document names what changed with a boolean such as
`deleted`, `revoked`, `removed` or `cleared`.

`project list`, `count`, `create`, `view`, `edit` and `delete`,
`account view` and `edit`, the `project agent-profile`, `project api-key`,
`project webhook`, `org oauth-client`, `org member`, `org invitation`,
`account avatar`, `account oauth` and `account service-key` commands,
`org leave`, `org billing payment-method status`, and the `project platform`
commands that read one response (`list`, `operation cancel`, `email dns`,
`imessage view`, `imessage assignment create`, `list`, `view` and `release`,
`telegram view`, and `whatsapp-business number list` and `number view`), the
ones that start an operation and wait for it (`sms purchase` and `delete`,
`imessage purchase`, `email connect` and `disconnect`, `telegram connect` and
`disconnect`, and `whatsapp-business number add --waba-id` and
`number delete`), the
`project platform voice` commands except
`profile delete`, `assignment set` and `assignment clear`, and
`project billing invoice list`, `billing operation view` and
`billing plan purchase`, print the API's response as Photon sends it instead:
a list keeps the API's envelope (`{"projects": [...], "nextPageToken": "..."}`,
`{"apiKeys": [...]}`, `{"destinations": [...], "nextPageToken": "..."}`,
`{"oauthClients": [...]}`, `{"serviceKeys": [...]}`,
`{"authorizedApplications": [...], "listMetadata": {...}}`, `{"scopes": [...]}`,
`{"platforms": [...]}`, `{"assignments": [...], "nextPageToken": "..."}`,
`{"resources": [...], "nextPageToken": "..."}`,
`{"profiles": [...], "nextPageToken": "..."}`, `{"members": [...]}`,
`{"invitations": [...]}`, `{"invoices": [...], "nextCursor": "..."}`), a
single item is the bare object, a deletion, release, removal or revocation
prints the API's response with no `deleted`, `left`, `released`, `removed` or
`revoked` boolean, and a field the API adds shows up in the JSON.
`org oauth-client update` reads the client before it changes it and prints the
response to the change,
`project delete` reads the project, to ask for its name, and prints the
response to the deletion, and `org member edit`, `org member remove` and
`org leave` read the organization's members (and `org leave` your account) to
find the membership's version, and print the response to the change.
`voice profile edit` and the `voice profile inbound` and `outbound` commands
read the profile for its version, and print the response to the change.
`billing operation view` and `billing plan purchase` wait for the billing
operation, and print the first answer Photon gives that isn't pending: for a
purchase, the purchase's own response when it's already final, or else the
operation read while waiting. A purchase whose operation failed prints the
error document instead, with `BILLING_OPERATION_REJECTED`. `operation view`
prints a failed operation like any other and exits `0`; its `status` is
`failed`.
The `project platform` commands that start an operation wait for it and print
the last operation read, `{"operation": {...}, "resource": {...}}`, with the
number, line, domain, bot or WhatsApp sender that a purchase, connect or
`number add` sets up in `resource`. A
purchase whose last operation read has no resource reads the operation once more
and prints that read. `sms delete`, `email disconnect`, `telegram disconnect`
and `whatsapp-business number delete` read the resource to check it (that the
number can send SMS, that it's an email domain or a Telegram bot, or that it's
an active WhatsApp Business sender), then release it with
`DELETE /v1/projects/{id}/platforms/resources/{resourceId}`. With `--no-wait`,
while the setup runs, `email connect` and `telegram connect` print Photon's
answer to the request that started it, which holds the DNS instructions or the
setup link in `operation.detail`. `email connect` prints the operation read
that holds the records instead when that answer doesn't hold them yet, and
`telegram connect` prints its read of the setup when it continues one already
in progress. An answer to a request, like the answer to a release that
has already ended, is the operation itself rather than an operation read, so the
command prints it under `operation`. It's the one place the CLI shapes these
documents, so the command keeps the operation read's `{operation, resource}`
shape on every path. `telegram connect` prints no `resumed` field when it
continues a setup already in progress: no Photon response carries it, and the
rule is to print the response as sent. Only its text says it's continuing, as
`kubectl apply -o json` prints the object and says `created` or `configured`
only in text.
`project agent-profile avatar upload` prints the profile Photon returns when it
commits the upload, and `account avatar upload` the account.

Thirty-five commands print a document the CLI defines. Photon answers the two
revocations and `voice profile delete` with no body, so they name what they
revoked or deleted, `voice assignment set` and `clear` change one line or
several, so they list one assignment for each `--resource-id`, as the table
below describes, `billing overview` puts the project's billing next to its
effective terms, `billing subscription cancel` and `resume` put the billing
operation of the plan change next to what it means for the subscription, or
say that nothing changed, `authorize` builds
an authorization request of its own, `project current`,
`project create --link` and `link` (and their deprecated old names,
`project create --use` and the hidden `project use`) put the API's project next
to where the CLI found it or saved it, `org show` and `org use` put the API's
organization next to where the CLI found it or saved it (`path` is there for
`source: "folder"` only), `org list` adds the ID of the organization the CLI
would use, `activeOrganizationId`, to the API's response, `org clear` and
`unlink` (and the hidden `project clear`, its deprecated old name) make no API
call, `payment-method setup` puts the API's checkout next to whether the
browser opened it, the `email`, `imessage`, `sms` and `telegram` `list` commands
put the API's resource page next to the provisioning operations a second read
finds, `whatsapp-business account list` lists the project's one account, or
none, so a project without one prints an empty list and exits `0`, as
`gh pr list --json` and `kubectl get -o json` do, and
`whatsapp-business number add` with `--source byon` or `--new-waba` hands off
to the dashboard, which no API response describes.
The sign-in commands describe the CLI's own sign-in: `login` and
`whoami` name the account it signs in as, `auth status` says where the
sign-in comes from and checks it with Photon when it can, and `logout` makes
no API call. `update` asks the package registry, or GitHub's releases for a
standalone binary, instead of Photon's API, and the `operation` commands read
the [local journal](/cli/operations):

| Command | JSON |
| --- | --- |
| `org oauth-client secret revoke` | `{"clientId": "...", "revoked": true}` |
| `account oauth connection revoke` | `{"applicationId": "...", "revoked": true}` |
| `project platform voice profile delete` | `{"deleted": true, "profileId": "...", "returnedToDefaultLineCount": 2}`: `returnedToDefaultLineCount` is the number of lines `--unassign-lines` returned to the default, `0` without it |
| `project platform voice assignment set`, `clear` | `{"assignments": [...]}`, one assignment for each `--resource-id`, in the order given. For a line the command changed, it's the assignment Photon returned for the change, or, after a `clear` that changes one line, which Photon answers with no body, the line as read after the change. A line that already uses the profile (for `clear`, one that's already unassigned) isn't changed, and shows as read before the change |
| `project platform email list`, `imessage list`, `sms list`, `telegram list` | `{"resources": [...], "nextPageToken": "...", "provisioning": [...]}`: the resource page as Photon sends it, pages merged as for the lists above, and under `provisioning` the operations still in progress that set up a resource of the listed kind, each as Photon sends it |
| `project platform whatsapp-business account list` | `{"accounts": [...]}`: the project's account as Photon sends it, or `{"accounts": []}` when none is connected |
| `project platform whatsapp-business number add --source byon`, `--new-waba` | `{"handoff": {"browserAttempted": true, "kind": "whatsapp-embedded-signup", "opened": true, "projectId": "...", "requiresHumanInteraction": true, "source": "byon", "url": "..."}}`: the dashboard URL and whether the browser opened it. With `--new-waba`, `source` is `"photon"`, `verificationOwner` is `"dashboard"` and `resource` is the SMS number as Photon sends it |
| `project billing overview` | `{"billing": {...}, "terms": {...}}`: the project's billing overview and its effective terms, each as Photon sends it. A plan change scheduled for the end of the period is in `terms.subscriptions[].scheduledPlanChange` |
| `project billing subscription cancel` | `{"operation": {...}, "subscription": {"cancellationScheduled": true, "cancelsAt": "...", "category": "..."}}`: `operation` is the billing operation of the move to the category's default plan, as `plan purchase` prints it, and `cancelsAt` is when the move takes effect, `null` when Photon doesn't say. When the category has no subscription or is already on its default plan, nothing changes: `{"subscription": {"cancellationScheduled": false, "cancelsAt": null, "category": "..."}}` |
| `project billing subscription resume` | `{"operation": {...}, "subscription": {"category": "...", "resumed": true}}`: `operation` is the billing operation of the change to the plan the project holds, as `plan purchase` prints it. When no change is scheduled for the end of the period, nothing changes: `{"subscription": {"category": "...", "resumed": false}}` |
| `org oauth-client authorize` | `{"authorization": {"authorizationUrl": "...", "clientId": "...", "issuer": "...", "nonce": "...", "opened": true, "redirectUri": "...", "scopes": [...], "state": "..."}}` |
| `project current` | `{"project": {...}, "source": "folder", "path": "..."}` |
| `project create --link` | `{"project": {...}, "gitignore": "added", "path": "...", "source": "folder"}`, or `{"project": {...}}` alone, with a warning on stderr, when the CLI can't save the folder's file (a folder error, or a project or organization ID that isn't canonical) |
| `org list` | `{"organizations": [...], "ownedOrganizationIds": [...], ..., "activeOrganizationId": "..."}`, without `activeOrganizationId` when no organization can be selected |
| `org show` | `{"organization": {...}, "source": "folder", "path": "..."}` |
| `org use` | `{"organization": {...}, "source": "stored"}` |
| `org clear` | `{"cleared": true}`, or `false` when no organization was stored |
| `org billing payment-method setup` | `{"checkout": {"checkoutUrl": "..."}, "opened": true}` |
| `link`, `project use` | `{"project": {...}, "gitignore": "added", "path": "...", "source": "folder"}` |
| `unlink`, `project clear` | `{"cleared": true, "path": "..."}`, or `{"cleared": false}` when the folder has no file, with `active` when `PHOTON_PROJECT_ID` or a file above still selects a project |
| `login` | `{"alreadyAuthenticated": false, "apiOrigin": "...", "email": "...", "name": "...", "storage": "keychain"}`, with `accountId`, `credentialKind: "account_service_key"` and `serviceKeyId` for `--service-key` |
| `whoami` | `{"accountId": "...", "email": "...", "name": "..."}` |
| `auth status` | `{"state": "authenticated", "apiOrigin": "...", "source": "stored", "storage": "keychain", "credentialKind": "device", "accountId": "...", "name": "...", "email": "...", "expiresAt": "..."}`. For a key in `PHOTON_API_TOKEN`, `source` is `"environment"` and `variable` names it, and `state` is `"unverified"`, with a `reason`, for a key Photon has no request to check. With `state` `"not_authenticated"` or `"unavailable"`, it still prints the document, with a `reason` when it has one, and exits with code `1` |
| `logout` | `{"orphanedCredential": false, "remoteSessionRevoked": false, "removed": true}`, with `removed: false` when there was no sign-in |
| `update` | `{"currentVersion": "...", "installer": "npm", "latestVersion": "...", "status": "updated"}`: `status` is `"up-to-date"`, `"update-available"` (with `--check`) or `"updated"`, and `installer` is `null` when nothing was installed |
| `operation list` | `{"operations": [...], "nextPageToken": "..."}`, without `nextPageToken` on the last page |
| `operation view` | `{"operation": {...}}` |
| `operation replay` | `{"operation": {"kind": "...", "operationId": "...", "status": "resolved"}, "result": {...}}`: the local operation it resolved and, under `result`, what the replayed command prints with `--json`, except for the results below |
| `operation remove` | `{"operationId": "...", "removed": true}` |

`operation replay` defines fourteen more. When it replays a `project delete`, a
`project webhook delete` or an iMessage `assignment release` that already went
through, Photon answers 404 and sends nothing to print, so the replay's
`result` names what is gone: `{"deleted": true, "projectId": "..."}` for the
project, `{"deleted": true, "destinationId": "..."}` for the destination and
`{"assignmentId": "...", "released": true}` for the assignment. The same goes
for an SMS `delete`, a WhatsApp Business `number delete`, an email
`disconnect` or a Telegram `disconnect` that Photon answers with 404, for the
number, sender, domain or bot, its release or the release's operation, when
the number, sender, domain or bot is gone (or the number, sender or domain is
retired): `{"deleted": true, "resourceId": "..."}` for the number or sender
and `{"disconnected": true, "resourceId": "..."}` for the domain or bot. A
replayed SMS `delete` or WhatsApp Business `number delete` that has no release
operation to read, because the number or sender was already retired, prints it
as Photon sends it: `{"resource": {...}}`. A replayed
`project create --link` can't save the folder's file, because the journal
doesn't record the folder, so its `result` is `{"project": {...}}` with the
API's project, and it prints the `photon link` step that saves the file (on
stderr with `--json`). When an `account service-key revoke` went through but
the CLI stopped before it finished printing the result, the replay doesn't
revoke the key again, and the journal doesn't keep Photon's response, so its
`result` names the key: `{"revoked": true, "serviceKeyId": "..."}`. The
journal records the plan change of a `billing subscription cancel` or
`resume` as the plan purchase it is, so its replay's `result` is what
`billing plan purchase` prints: the billing operation as Photon sends it,
which the command itself prints under `operation`, without `subscription`.
A replayed `email connect` or `telegram connect` doesn't wait for Photon to
verify the records or for the bot to connect. Its `result` is Photon's answer
as the replay got it, with the setup under `operation`. While the setup is
still running, that's what the command prints with `--no-wait`: the DNS
records or the setup link, under `operation`. When Photon sends the resource
beside the setup, as it does for an email domain whose setup has finished,
it's under `resource`.

## Errors

Without `--json`, an error is text on stderr. With `--json`, it's the one JSON
object on stdout, in place of the result, and the exit code is the same as
without `--json`. The one exception is a command that fails after its result
is already on stdout, or while writing it there: the error object goes to
stderr, so stdout never holds two objects.

<Tabs>
  <Tab title="Terminal">
    ```console
    $ photon project platform sms purchase --yes --project pho_prj_01k9yq3m7d2c8x4v6b5n0p1r2s
    Error: Buying a number needs a payment card on file. (ENTITLEMENT_REQUIRED, card_required)
    Request ID: req_01example
    Docs: https://photon.codes/docs/problems/entitlement-required
    Your organization's billing or plan has to change first. The docs page says how.
    ```
  </Tab>
  <Tab title="JSON">
    ```console
    $ photon project platform sms purchase --yes --project pho_prj_01k9yq3m7d2c8x4v6b5n0p1r2s --json
    {"error":{"code":"ENTITLEMENT_REQUIRED","message":"Buying a number needs a payment card on file.","reason":"card_required","requestId":"req_01example","retryable":false,"docsUrl":"https://photon.codes/docs/problems/entitlement-required","problem":{"type":"https://photon.codes/docs/problems/entitlement-required","title":"Entitlement Required","status":402,"code":"ENTITLEMENT_REQUIRED","reason":"card_required","detail":"Buying a number needs a payment card on file."}}}
    ```
  </Tab>
</Tabs>

| Field | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `code` | Stable identifier. Branch on this. For an API error it's the API's own `code`, unchanged. |
| `message` | What went wrong, in plain words. For an API error it's the problem's `detail`. When Photon refuses a key in `PHOTON_API_TOKEN` with HTTP 403, it names the key's kind and the variable instead, and `detail` holds Photon's words, except for commands that handle secrets, which leave them out. The wording can change in any release. |
| `reason` | The API's finer-grained cause, such as `card_required`, when the API sends one. |
| `requestId` | Photon's ID for the request. Quote it when you contact support. |
| `retryable` | Whether the same request can succeed unchanged, after `retryAfterSeconds` when that's present. |
| `retryAfterSeconds` | How long the API asked you to wait before trying again. |
| `docsUrl` | The page that explains the error: the problem's type page for an API error, otherwise this section. |
| `detail` | More about one of the CLI's own errors. When Photon refuses a key in `PHOTON_API_TOKEN` with HTTP 403, Photon's own words, except for commands that handle secrets. |
| `issues` | For invalid input, each field that was rejected and why. |
| `next` | Ordered steps that fix the error. Present only when there's a concrete fix. |
| `problem` | The API's [RFC 9457](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9457) problem details, redacted like the rest of the output. Absent when the API wasn't reached. |
| `transportCode` | Why a request got no response, for `API_CONNECTION_ERROR` and the other errors where a server couldn't be reached: `TimeoutError` when it didn't answer in time, otherwise the network error's code, such as `ECONNREFUSED`. |
| `operationId`, `idempotencyKey`, `replayable` | Present when the failure left a [recovery record](/cli/operations). |

Each step in `next` has a `description`, plus a `run` with the full command or a
`url` to open when there is one, and `person: true` when only a person can do
it, such as adding a payment card:

```json
{"error":{"code":"COMMAND_MOVED","message":"\"photon project member\" moved to \"photon org member\".","retryable":false,"docsUrl":"https://photon.codes/docs/cli/output#errors","next":[{"description":"Use the new command","run":"photon org member list --json"}]}}
```

For an API error, `next` holds the fix the API sends in the problem's
`remediation`. The CLI doesn't write steps of its own for API errors.

For invalid input (`ARGUMENT_VALIDATION`), `next` holds the steps written for
that error. When a command finds invalid input and the error has no steps of
its own, it gets one step instead: the command with `--help`, which lists its
valid options. An unknown command, an unknown option, a missing argument or an
ID in the wrong format is rejected before the command runs, and gets one step
without a `run`: run the command with `--help` for its valid options. A
`--waba-id` that isn't digits is the exception: when it's the only input
rejected, its error gives the command that lists the project's connected
WhatsApp Business Account. A full API key given to `project api-key revoke`
isn't rejected before the command runs: the command takes it, and its error
gives the command to run with the key's ID.

The terminal shows the same fields as text: the message with the code and
reason, any issues, the steps under `Next`, then the request ID and the docs
link. Each step shows its description, then its command or link:

```console
$ photon project member list
Error: "photon project member" moved to "photon org member". (COMMAND_MOVED)
Next: Use the new command: photon org member list
Docs: https://photon.codes/docs/cli/output#errors
```

An unknown command or long option also gets one line in text naming the
closest name at the same level, when one is within two edits. The CLI never
runs it, and the JSON error has no suggestion:

```console
$ photon project lst
Error: The command contains invalid input. (ARGUMENT_VALIDATION)
Detail: Command not found: lst
Did you mean list?
Next: Run the command with --help for valid options.
Docs: https://photon.codes/docs/cli/output#errors
```

`problem` appears only with `--debug`. An error with no step ends with a
general hint chosen by `retryable`, the HTTP status and whether the error has a
detail. A retryable error that's safe to repeat gets none.

| Case | Hint |
| --- | --- |
| Retryable, but the command was a write sent without an idempotency key | Trying again can work, but this command isn't safe to repeat: check first whether it took effect. |
| 402 | Your organization's billing or plan has to change first. The docs page says how. |
| Any other 4xx that isn't retryable, with a detail | Review the error detail and correct the request before retrying. |
| Any other 4xx that isn't retryable, with no detail | Check the docs page for this error and correct the request before retrying. |
| A 5xx that isn't retryable | This failed on Photon's side. If it keeps happening, contact support with the request ID. |

<Warning>
  `retryable` and `replayable` answer different questions and can legitimately
  disagree. A retryable transport error on a non-replayable mutation means
  "try the command again"; a non-retryable error on a replayable mutation means
  "use [`photon operation replay`](/cli/operations#replay)".
</Warning>

### When stdout fails

When the CLI can't write its output to stdout, as with a full disk, it prints
one line on stderr and exits `1`. With `--json`, the line is the error
object:

```console
$ photon --version > /dev/full
Error: Photon could not write the result to standard output. (OUTPUT_WRITE_FAILED)
$ photon operation list --json > /dev/full
{"error":{"code":"OUTPUT_WRITE_FAILED","message":"Photon could not write the result to standard output.","retryable":false,"docsUrl":"https://photon.codes/docs/cli/output#errors"}}
```

When the reader has closed the pipe, the command exits `1` without a message,
and so does a command whose stderr can't be written either. A command that
keeps its result in the [operation journal](/cli/operations#record-lifecycle)
until stdout takes it, such as a new API key, reports a closed pipe too, since
running it again would make a second key: its error names the replay that
prints the result again.

## Prompts and confirmation

The CLI asks a question only when stdin, stdout and stderr are all terminals,
`CI` isn't on, and there's no `--json`, `--no-input` or `PHOTON_NO_INPUT`.
Questions go to stderr. When it can't ask, the command fails before it changes
anything, names the option that supplies the answer, and says why it couldn't
ask.

A command that costs money or can't be undone asks first. `--yes`, or `-y`,
confirms without being asked, and `--yes=false` is the same as leaving it out.
At a terminal the command asks `[y/N]`; deleting a project means typing its
name. Answering no changes nothing, prints `Cancelled.` on stderr and exits
`1`. Without a terminal and without `--yes`, the command changes nothing and
fails with `CONFIRMATION_REQUIRED` and exit code `2`. The message says what
would have happened, and `next` is the same command with `--yes`:

```console
$ photon project delete pho_prj_01k9yq3m7d2c8x4v6b5n0p1r2s < /dev/null
Error: This would delete project "Alpha Project" (pho_prj_01k9yq3m7d2c8x4v6b5n0p1r2s). Confirm it with --yes. (CONFIRMATION_REQUIRED)
Detail: Photon can't ask because standard input is not a terminal.
Next: Run it again with --yes: photon project delete pho_prj_01k9yq3m7d2c8x4v6b5n0p1r2s --yes
Docs: https://photon.codes/docs/cli/output#errors
```

`--force` is only for overriding a safety check: `login --force` replaces a
healthy session, and `logout --force` removes the local files of a credential
the CLI can't read.

A command reads stdin only when you ask it to: `login --service-key`, or an
option given `-`, such as `--token-file -`. Piped input is never read
otherwise; a command that needs a value it wasn't given fails and names the
option.

## Exit codes

| Code | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `0` | Success, including an empty list |
| `1` | Failure, including a key the API refuses as the wrong kind, a confirmation you declined, and `auth status` when it finds no sign-in (`not_authenticated`) or can't check one (`unavailable`) |
| `2` | Invalid usage or input, including a missing confirmation (`CONFIRMATION_REQUIRED`), a missing project (`PROJECT_NOT_SELECTED`), a [moved command](/cli/organizations#moved-commands) (`COMMAND_MOVED`), a Photon CLI 1.0 command (`LEGACY_COMMAND`), a Photon CLI 1.0 credential (`LEGACY_TOKEN`) and `photon login` while `PHOTON_API_TOKEN` is set (`AUTH_ENVIRONMENT_KEY_SET`) |
| `4` | Sign-in needed, except for `auth status`, which exits with `1`: no credential, an expired or revoked one (including a key in `PHOTON_API_TOKEN`), or a `photon login` code that expired (`NOT_AUTHENTICATED`, `AUTH_SESSION_EXPIRED`, `AUTH_REFRESH_FAILED`, `AUTH_REAUTHENTICATION_REQUIRED`, `AUTH_DEVICE_CODE_EXPIRED`, or the API's HTTP 401) |
| `129` | Ended by `SIGHUP` |
| `130` | Interrupted by <kbd>Ctrl-C</kbd> (`SIGINT`) |
| `143` | Ended by `SIGTERM` |

Exit code `2` is what a script gets when it leaves out `--yes` on a command
that confirms. It's a usage error, not an approval: nothing was changed.

No exit code means "a person must act": that's a step in `next` with
`person: true`. Whether trying again can help is `retryable`, not an exit
code.

A signal ends a command with 128 plus the signal's number: `130` for
<kbd>Ctrl-C</kbd> (`SIGINT`), `143` for `SIGTERM` and `129` for `SIGHUP`. Each
winds the command down the same way: it stops, prints its interrupted error
(`COMMAND_INTERRUPTED`, or a command's own, such as `LOGIN_INTERRUPTED`), and
leaves the [operation journal](/cli/operations) as <kbd>Ctrl-C</kbd> leaves it. A
second signal ends the process at once with 128 plus that signal's number. A
GitHub Actions cancel sends `SIGINT`, then `SIGTERM` if the step is still
running 7.5 seconds later, so a command that hasn't finished winding down by
then ends in `143`.

## Help

`--help` prints a command's help, and so does `photon help` before the
command. A group run without one of its commands prints its help too:

```sh
photon project list --help
photon help project list
photon project
```

With `--json`, a group run on its own prints the same reference as
`--help --json` (see [Beta commands](#beta-commands)), and `photon --json`
prints the CLI's name and version.

## Stability

From 3.0.0, the 3.x promise covers only what the CLI defines itself: command
names, flags, exit codes, error codes, and the JSON the CLI defines (errors,
`login`, `whoami`, `update` and workflow commands). Output that comes from
the API follows the API: the golden tests on SDK update pull requests catch a
change, and the release notes name it. A workflow command is one that makes
more than one call, or reads local state such as `org list`'s active
organization. When a workflow command prints an API response as Photon sends
it, as `project delete` and `org member edit` do, what's fixed is which
response it prints, and the response's fields follow the API. For a command
that prints an API response, the golden tests type the response against
Photon's API SDK, so the pull request that updates the SDK fails when it
removes or changes a field those tests use.

The JSON the CLI defines is the [error object](#errors), the documents in the
table under [Envelopes](#envelopes) and, from the prose there, the
`{"operation": {...}, "resource": {...}}` and `{"operation": {...}}`
documents of the `project platform` commands that start an operation, the
`--help --json` reference and `photon --json`. The
[debug trace](#debug-traces) isn't part of it: its shape may change between
releases. What's fixed doesn't change or go away in a 3.x release, but a 3.x
release may add fields, commands, flags, error codes and enum values. So a
script should ignore a field it doesn't know and expect an enum value it
hasn't seen. A [beta command](#beta-commands) can still change itself.

## Beta commands

Commands that call API operations Photon hasn't finished documenting are
labelled beta. Their help description ends in `(beta)`, in the command's own
help and in its parent's command list:

```console
$ photon org --help
  ...
  leave                 Leave the current organization (beta)
  list                  List your organizations (beta)
```

`--help --json` prints a command's reference as one JSON object on stdout, with
`"beta": true` on beta commands:

```console
$ photon org list --help --json
{"beta":true,"description":"List your organizations (beta)","options":[...],"path":["photon","org","list"]}
```

Beta means the command itself may change in a 3.x release: its name, its
flags, or whether it exists. A command loses the label once every operation it
calls is published in `@photon-ai/api` with a description, and each label is
reviewed every 90 days.

## Debug traces

Global `--debug` buffers sanitized HTTP exchanges and emits one ordered trace
to stderr when the command finishes:

```sh
photon --debug project list
photon --debug --json project list 2> trace.json
```

A successful `--json` command keeps its single result object on stdout and
writes a separate `{"debug":{"http":...}}` object to stderr. A failed JSON
command stays one error object, with the exchanges and sanitized cause chain
under `error.debug`. Commands that make no HTTP requests emit no trace.

Each exchange records the method, sanitized endpoint, query-parameter names,
safe headers, JSON bodies when safe, status, request ID, and duration. Long
traces keep the first three and latest 22 exchanges and report how many were
dropped in between.

<Warning>
  Review debug output before sharing it. Photon redacts authorization headers,
  cookies, API-key headers, query values, tokens, device and invitation codes,
  payment capabilities, presigned upload paths and form data, 10DLC profiles
  and campaign input, Call Registry profiles, tax identifiers, phone numbers,
  OTP and PIN values, registration feedback, and personal fields — but debug
  payloads are diagnostic and their shape may change between releases.
</Warning>

## Text from the API

Text output writes every control and format character in a string from the
API as visible text, so a name or message can't move the cursor, change
colors, break a line or reorder what follows. Control characters print in
caret notation (`\u001b[31m` prints as `^[[31m`, a line break as `^J`), and
format characters such as a right-to-left override print as `\u{202e}`.
Joiners are format characters too, so an emoji sequence such as 👩‍💻 prints as
`👩\u{200d}💻`. JSON keeps the string as the API sent it: the encoder escapes
U+0000 to U+001F, quotes and backslashes, and writes every other character as
it is.

## Color

Human output uses compact tables and labeled detail views. ANSI styling is
enabled only for terminal streams, and text labels and symbols always carry the
same meaning without color.

Styling is disabled for JSON, for redirected output, for `TERM=dumb`, for a
non-empty `NO_COLOR` or `NO_COLORS`, and for `--no-color`. `FORCE_COLOR=1`
enables it for a non-terminal stream unless `--no-color` or `--json` disables
it. An explicit `--color` styles a terminal even when `NO_COLOR`, `NO_COLORS`,
`NODE_DISABLE_COLORS` or `FORCE_COLOR=0` is set, unless `TERM` is `dumb`.

## Environment variables

| Variable | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| `PHOTON_API_TOKEN` | A Photon key that every command uses instead of the stored sign-in. The CLI saves no credential for it. Empty is ignored. See [Authentication](/cli/authentication#use-a-key-from-the-environment). |
| `PHOTON_PROJECT_ID` | Selects the project, below `--project` and above the folder's `.photon/project.json`. Empty is ignored. |
| `PHOTON_ORGANIZATION_ID` | Selects the organization, below `--org` and above the folder's project and `org use`. Empty is ignored. |
| `NO_COLOR`, `NO_COLORS` | Any non-empty value disables ANSI styling. |
| `FORCE_COLOR=1` | Enables styling on a non-terminal stream. |
| `TERM=dumb` | Disables styling. |
| `HTTPS_PROXY`, `HTTP_PROXY` | The proxy to send requests through: `HTTPS_PROXY` for `https://` URLs and `HTTP_PROXY` for `http://` URLs. Every request to Photon's API is an `https://` one, and so are the daily check for a new version and the downloads of `photon update`. The lower-case names work too. When the proxy refuses the connection, or its name doesn't resolve, the error names the proxy, such as `The proxy proxy.example.com:3128 could not be reached.` |
| `NO_PROXY` | Comma-separated hosts to reach directly, without the proxy, such as `localhost,.internal.example.com`. |
| `NODE_EXTRA_CA_CERTS` | A PEM file of extra certificate authorities to trust, such as your company proxy's. Node.js and the standalone binary read it when they start. |
| `PHOTON_NO_INPUT` | When on, the CLI never asks a question, as with `--no-input`. |
| `PHOTON_NO_UPDATE_NOTIFIER` | When on, the CLI turns off the daily check for a new version and the notice it shows. |
| `CI` | When on, the CLI never asks a question, shows no spinner, and turns off the daily check for a new version and its notice. |

`PHOTON_NO_INPUT`, `PHOTON_NO_UPDATE_NOTIFIER` and `CI` are on when set to
anything except empty, `0` or `false`.
